      <h1>Why Teams Do Not Adopt HubSpot</h1>
      <p class="guide-dek">Adoption is usually an architecture problem, not a training problem.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main id="main-content" class="guide-main" style="--accent:#06245B;--accent-soft:#F1F5F9;--accent-border:#CBD5E1;">
    <div class="container guide-layout">
      <article class="guide-card">
        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field notes</div>
        <h2>The practical breakdown</h2>
        <p class="intro">When teams do not adopt HubSpot, the instinct is to schedule another training session. Training helps, but it cannot overcome a portal that asks people to duplicate work, hides the next action, or uses language that does not match how the business operates.</p>
        <section><h3>Remove friction from the daily workflow</h3><p>Adoption starts with the record a person opens every day. Make the important information visible, reduce unnecessary fields, and turn common actions into a clear path. If the CRM is slower than a spreadsheet, people will work around it.</p><ul><li>Design views around jobs to be done, not every available field.</li><li>Show the next action, owner, and timing in the working view.</li><li>Remove duplicate properties, unused stages, and confusing labels.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Document the why, not just the click path</h3><p>An SOP should explain what to do, when to do it, and why the data matters. Pair short written steps with examples and a Loom-style walkthrough for the moments that cause the most confusion.</p><ul><li>Document the trigger, action, owner, and expected outcome.</li><li>Use real examples from the team’s current process.</li><li>Keep SOPs close to the workflow and version them when the process changes.</li></ul></section><section><h3>Measure confidence and behavior</h3><p>Login counts do not equal adoption. Look for consistent stage updates, timely follow-up, complete records, and reliable handoffs. Ask users where the system creates workarounds, then fix the highest-friction moments first.</p><ul><li>Review usage by workflow and outcome.</li><li>Create a simple path for users to report friction.</li><li>Celebrate clean handoffs and useful data, not activity for its own sake.</li></ul></section>
        <section>
          <h3>What to change this week</h3>
          <p>Use this short checklist to turn the article into an operating improvement.</p>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Daily workflow mapped</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Unnecessary fields removed</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>SOP owner assigned</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Examples recorded</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Friction feedback channel open</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Adoption signals reviewed</div></div></div>
        </section>
        <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Adoption is the result of a system that respects how people work. Build the workflow first, then train the team inside a process they can trust.</p></div>
